        "math_academy_id": "q-29379",
        "problem": "Which of the following is the graph of  $y=-{(\\frac{1}{10})}^{x-3}?$",
        "worked_solution": "To plot the given function, we follow these steps:\n\n- We take the curve $y={(\\frac{1}{10})}^{x}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007409/assets/d61528c7868baa92a1adbb2c4b4b23f5c975df80dbbabfeb449ad73acea97802.png)\n\n \n\n- Horizontally shift it by $3$ units to the right to give $y={(\\frac{1}{10})}^{x-3}.$ The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,1)$ to $(3,1)$ but the horizontal asymptote stays at $y=0.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007409/assets/3a4e3b5a90900d204c3265187d01343233bcaa76d811276464742fa447e4543a.png)\n\n- Then, reflect it on the $x$-axis to give $y=-{(\\frac{1}{10})}^{x-3}.$ The point $(3,1)$ moves to $(3,-1)$ and the horizontal asymptote stays at $y=0.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007409/assets/0936975d549f0f94f4c4139b9543142308c2cc56d38d1bba2c94dc6c681b3d99.png)",
